1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Eight billion people.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:08,400
In 2023, 130 countries and areas

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,000
had fertility below the level
that replaces each generation.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,100
Yet 84 are still growing.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:25,100
66 have more births than deaths;
18 grow only because more people arrive than leave.

6
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,700
One number can't explain that.

7
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,900
We are Team UNALSta-R.

8
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,300
Three questions.

9
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:36,500
One: how do births, deaths and migration relate,
by region, country and income?

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:41,300
Two: does one indicator tell the whole story,
or only several together?

11
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:47,300
Three: do countries share one road at different
speeds, and what should the UN watch?

12
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,900
Population change is bookkeeping:
births minus deaths, plus net migration.

13
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:55,700
In today's high-income countries,
net migration accounted for

14
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:01,200
about seven in ten of the growth since 2000.

15
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,800
Elsewhere, growth came almost
entirely from births exceeding deaths.

16
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:08,200
Blue: more births than deaths, and growing,

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:14,300
including 22 below replacement
that grow with no net arrivals.

18
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,900
Light blue: deaths exceed births,
but arrivals keep it growing.

19
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:22,300
Orange: deaths exceed births, and it shrinks.

20
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:26,900
Purple: births exceed deaths,
but net departures make it shrink.

21
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:31,200
Migration is also the least certain lever.

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:39,700
For the Emirates in 2050, the UN's 95 percent
range is nine to thirty-three million.

23
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:45,700
Set each country's clock to when its
fertility fell below four children,

24
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,700
and the curves line up.

25
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:50,900
The road is shared; the speed is not.

26
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:59,100
China went from five to three
children in 5 years; Egypt took 33.

27
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:02,500
Survival analysis:
child deaths started falling first

28
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:08,000
in 106 of 109 high-fertility countries.

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,700
The gap was typically at least 22 years.

30
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:14,900
A within-country panel regression

31
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:18,100
finds this link smaller
than comparing countries suggests.

32
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:21,300
A sequence in time, not proof of cause.

33
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:25,900
Question one, answered:
child deaths fell first and births followed,

34
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:29,100
at each country's pace;
latest in low-income countries.

35
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,900
The link is clearest in sub-Saharan Africa.

36
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:36,700
Migration moves on its own,
alike among neighbours only in the Gulf,

37
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,100
and weighs most in high-income countries.

38
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:41,100
Question two.

39
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,500
Fertility alone captures the slow story:

40
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:49,400
about three quarters of the differences
in how old populations are.

41
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:54,900
Only part of today's growth,
and almost none of migration.

42
00:02:54,900 --> 00:03:01,800
Fertility alone finds only one in eight
of each country's closest look-alikes.

43
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:06,000
Principal component analysis:
countries differ in two separate ways.

44
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,400
Fertility tracks the first,
the transition to fewer births and deaths,

45
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,500
but misses migration.

46
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,100
Blend indicators into one score:

47
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:21,800
the weights can shift one country
in five by more than twenty places.

48
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,400
Question two, answered: no.

49
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,600
The Emirates and Belarus share the same fertility;

50
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:32,100
one grows almost four percent a year,
the other shrinks.

51
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,500
The story emerges only when transition
and migration are read together.

52
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:38,500
Question three.

53
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:42,900
K-means groups countries' seventy-year
paths into three stretches of one road,

54
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,700
plus a migration-led group, mostly Gulf states.

55
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:50,100
Early movers are projected to grow
only through migration, or shrink.

56
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:52,500
Fast movers, India, China and Indonesia,

57
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:58,800
are projected to age
from 30 to 40 in about 32 years.

58
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,400
About as fast as Early movers did, or faster.

59
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,600
Recent movers have the highest
child mortality today.

60
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:10,600
Countries grew closer in life expectancy,
but further apart in age.

61
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:14,800
Question three, answered:
mostly one road, at different speeds.

62
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:21,200
For the UN: watch child survival
where it is off pace for the 2030 goal.

63
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:25,200
Watch ageing where fertility
is low but populations still grow.

64
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,400
Watch migration data
where growth depends on arrivals.

65
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:31,400
Following ASA guidelines, projections are scenarios;

66
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:34,200
hypotheses were fixed before the final run.

67
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:36,200
These are patterns, not causes;

68
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:40,600
every number and our AI use
are documented in our notebook.

69
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:44,000
One number can't tell eight billion stories.

70
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
But births, deaths and migration,
read together region by region,

71
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,200
can tell the UN where to look next.
